  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Temperatures will sink below seasonal norms for\nthe next several days. Frost Advisory in effect inland tonight \nand Freeze Watch remains in effect Saturday night.\n\nCold advection will maintain below normal temperatures into \nnext week. A deep upper trough moving across tonight may squeeze\nout a few light showers over southeast NC late this evening. \nTemperatures drop into the 30s tonight and there is chance for \npatchy to areas of frost inland by Saturday morning and \ntherefore a Frost Advisory has been issued. However, confidence \nis low in frost formation due to boundary layer winds remaining \nslightly elevated due to the aforementioned trough. If the winds\ngo light/calm anywhere overnight (especially sheltered areas), \nthe temperatures will quickly drop and frost will develop. If \nwinds remain elevated through morning, temperatures will likely \nremain in the upper 30s. Since this might not be uniform (some \nlocations may go calm while others remain mixed), the Frost \nAdvisory was issued to cover the potential.\n\nSaturday night remains the coldest night of the forecast, with \nforecasted temps at or just below freezing away from the coast \n(confidence is low in freeze potential for New Hanover and \nBrunswick counties as well). The Freeze Watch remains in place \nfor Saturday night and will evaluate upgrading to a Warning with\ntonight's forecast package. Daytime temps this weekend will \nstruggle to hit 60F.\n\nLow temps remain in the 30s into middle of next week with high \npressure moving overhead allowing for good radiational cooling \neach night. High temps near 60F Monday will slowly warm each day\nduring the week.\n\nBesides the potential for spotty showers tonight, forecast remains \ndry through next week.",
